7
00:00:25.825 --> 00:00:30.767
The tempting shortcut is insufficient: Generic access control, one robustness score,

8
00:00:30.767 --> 00:00:36.158
static red teaming, accuracy on clean data, or a single defense cannot

9
00:00:36.158 --> 00:00:37.955
characterize a model attack.

10
00:00:38.555 --> 00:00:44.670
The chapter's core claim is this: A learned model should receive security authority

11
00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:49.845
only through a versioned model-threat contract and attack/defense ledger that binds

12
00:00:49.845 --> 00:00:54.078
checkpoint identity, lifecycle stage, attacker knowledge and capability, surface,

13
00:00:54.078 --> 00:00:55.960
budget, objective, adaptation, transfer,

14
00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:00.888
observed effect, detection, mitigation, utility cost, recovery, residual,

15
00:01:00.888 --> 00:01:05.475
and disclosure; clean accuracy, attack failure, benchmark robustness,

16
00:01:05.475 --> 00:01:10.636
red-team coverage, or formal certification alone establishes neither general

17
00:01:10.636 --> 00:01:12.930
robustness nor secure deployment.

18
00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:17.691
Version the threat model by model family, exact checkpoint,

19
00:01:17.691 --> 00:01:22.603
data and adaptation lineage, modality, lifecycle stage, access, attacker goal,

20
00:01:22.603 --> 00:01:25.550
knowledge, budget, and prohibited real-world effects.

21
00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:30.138
Maintain separate evasion, poisoning, backdoor, safeguard-bypass, extraction,

22
00:01:30.138 --> 00:01:34.993
inversion, transfer, adaptive, multimodal, and agentic attack lanes

23
00:01:34.993 --> 00:01:37.420
